/P 407 0 R
/T (Differences:)
/E (Differences:)
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
778 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1620 0 R
/E (The extent to which FoMO plays as a mediating role differs with varying factors: Whilst our main case study \(Hussain et al., 2025\) states that FoMO affects moderate users of social media as much as extreme users, therefore making FoMO a highly influential factor, other readings like Khalaf et al \(2023\) and Giancola et al \(2025\) state that the effect of FoMO is greater on extreme users. )
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
779 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1621 0 R
/E (Age as a confounding factor for high usage of social media: Giancola et al \(2025\) also found that the greater the age of adolescents, the more intense the feelings of FoMO are, and therefore the greater the usage of social media. On the other hand, Messena & Everri \(2023\) argue that the use of social media is positively correlated with younger ages because of the lower ability to self-restrain themselves compared to older individuals.)
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
